Practice Problems — Topic 9
Q1. Solder joint quality identification:
Describe the appearance of a correct solder joint and three types of defective joints. For each defective joint, state: (1) visual appearance, (2) likely cause, (3) effect on circuit operation if not caught, (4) repair procedure.
Q2. Assembly sequence planning:
For your flyback capstone PCB, create a detailed assembly sequence. List all 25+ components in the exact order you would solder them, explaining why each group comes before or after the next. Consider component height, thermal mass, and access difficulty.
Q3. DPAK MOSFET soldering analysis:
The STD5NK60Z MOSFET in DPAK has a large thermal tab that must make good thermal and electrical contact with the PCB pad.
(a) Why is the thermal tab solder joint more thermally critical than the three pin joints?
(b) If the tab solder joint has a 0.1mm air gap under 50% of the tab area: estimate the thermal resistance penalty. (Each 1mm² of air gap ≈ 4°C/W additional thermal resistance. Tab area = 10mm × 8mm = 80mm². 50% air gap = 40mm².)
(c) How does insufficient flux cause tab soldering problems?
(d) Describe how you would verify the tab is fully soldered from the visual appearance of the bottom of the PCB.
(e) If you suspect the tab is not properly soldered after the circuit is already assembled: describe the rework procedure without removing other components.
Q4. Pre-power inspection checklist:
Before applying power to your assembled flyback PCB for the first time, create a 15-point inspection checklist. For each item, describe: (1) what you check, (2) how you check it (visual, multimeter, etc.), (3) what result indicates pass.
Critical items to include: bulk capacitor polarity, MOSFET orientation, bridge rectifier diode orientations, output capacitor polarity, feedback divider resistance values, R_sense value, gate drive continuity, primary-secondary isolation resistance.
Q5. Soldering problem-solving:
During assembly of your flyback converter, you encounter these problems. For each, describe the cause and solution:
(a) After soldering the 100µF/400V electrolytic capacitor, you notice it is installed backwards (+ to PGND instead of VIN)
(b) While soldering the UC3842 socket, solder flowed between pins 5 and 6, creating a bridge
(c) An 0805 resistor tombstoned during soldering — it is standing vertically on one end
(d) After soldering the bridge rectifier diodes, you measure 0Ω resistance between the AC input terminals (they should be isolated from each other when the circuit is not powered)
(e) The secondary Schottky diode pad lifted off the PCB during desoldering (you were trying to replace it). The SMB pad is now gone.
